- Ableton stores DJ-mixer-style bus automation two ways depending on era/schema: modern `<AutomationEnvelope>` on shared Zone A/B Return tracks (newer files), or older inline per-parameter `<ArrangerAutomation>` drawn directly on a song track's own device (2015-era files) - missing the second mechanism entirely is what caused a real false claim ("12 files mixed live, nothing captured") this session, caught by peer review, not self-caught.
- `TrackSendHolder`'s own `Id=` attribute is not a reliable positional index for resolving which zone/bus a send targets - must resolve by order of appearance.
- Dropbox's own internal sync-engine database (`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Dropbox\instance1\sync\nucleus.sqlite3`) can be read directly (copy first, it's actively written) to find what's actually queued, much faster than a filesystem scan on a large Dropbox tree with Cloud Filter reparse-point overhead.
- PowerShell's `Set-Content -Path` treats `[...]` in a path as a wildcard character class - a bulk NTFS alternate-data-stream write across ~5,000 files with bracketed folder names (`[Defected]` etc., common in this business's label-tagged project folders) silently failed on nearly all of them until switched to `-LiteralPath`.

